    91	form (`B0:537-548`, confirmed in `REV:89,151`): in the Keller target coordinates
    92	each component is parametrized at leading order by
    93	
    94	\[
    95	 (A_i t^{m_i r}+\cdots, B_i t^{m_i s}+\cdots),\qquad \gcd(r,s)=1,
    96	\]
    97	
    98	with the same $(r,s)$, and all components meet the same physical point of
    99	$L_\infty$, denoted $P_\infty$.  The multiplier $m_D$, the later series, and the
   100	denominator-shedding sequence are unpinned.  In particular, this leading
   101	statement is not a ROW-NF and does not decide whether the infinity braid is torus
   102	or cable.
   103	
   104	## 2. The $S_5$ monodromy problem and the failed $S_4$ resolvent transfer
   105	
   106	The reviewed monodromy is a necessary representation
   107	
   108	\[
   109	 \rho:\pi_1(\mathbb C^2-D)\twoheadrightarrow S_5,\qquad
   110	 \rho(\text{meridian})\sim(2,1,1,1).                              \tag{2.1}
   111	\]
   112	
   113	All other component meridians are trivial, so the original representation factors
   114	through this one-curve complement (`B0:550-569`; `REV:64-66,146-152`).
   115	Transitivity comes from connectedness of the five-sheeted cover, and a transitive
   116	group generated by transpositions is $S_5$: its transpositions are edges of a
   117	connected graph on five vertices.  At every self-double point the two branch
   118	meridians are **disjoint** transpositions.  Equal transpositions fix three letters,
   119	and transpositions sharing one letter fix two, whereas the reviewed local fibre has
   120	one fixed letter.  The incidence correction in §1 merely changes where that fixed
   121	letter is represented; it does not alter this conclusion.
   122	
   123	### 2.1 No nonabelian quotient resolvent
   124	
   125	The normal subgroups of $S_5$ are $1,A_5,S_5$.  Indeed $A_5$ is simple, and a
   126	normal subgroup meeting $A_5$ trivially would inject into $S_5/A_5$; a nontrivial
   127	order-two normal subgroup would then be central, contrary to $Z(S_5)=1$.  Therefore
   128	
   129	\[
   130	                         S_5/A_5\simeq C_2                         \tag{2.2}
   131	\]
   132	
   133	is the only nontrivial proper quotient.  There is no $S_5\twoheadrightarrow S_3$,
   134	and no analogue of $S_4\twoheadrightarrow S_4/V_4\simeq S_3$.  In particular,
   135	the N=4 chain “transpositions to transpositions in a triple cover to normal-triple-
   136	plane classification” stops at its first arrow.
   137	
   138	There are useful *permutation resolvents*, but they are not quotients:
   139	
   140	| action | degree | image of a natural transposition | consequence |
   141	|---|---:|---|---|
   142	| natural $S_5/S_4$ | 5 | $1^3 2$ | the original quintic cover |
   143	| sign $S_5/A_5$ | 2 | $2$ | double cover; loses the $A_5$ layer |
   144	| six Sylow-5 subgroups | 6 | $2^3$ | classical sextic resolvent, faithful |
   145	| two-element subsets | 10 | $1^4 2^3$ | faithful degree-ten resolvent |
   146	
   147	For the degree-six line, a stabilizer is the order-20 normalizer of a 5-cycle.
   148	A transposition normalizes no Sylow-5 subgroup (the involutions in that affine
   149	normalizer have natural type $1\,2^2$), hence has no fixed point and type $2^3$.
   150	For the degree-ten line, $\{1,2\}$ and the three pairs disjoint from it are fixed,
   151	while $\{1,j\}$ and $\{2,j\}$ are swapped for $j=3,4,5$.  Neither action produces a
   152	simply branched triple cover.  Calling either one “the $S_5$ quotient” would
   153	confuse an associated cover with a normal quotient.
   154	
   155	The sign factor is exact but weak.  It makes the Galois closure factor through the
   156	double plane fixed by $A_5$; the remaining layer has group $A_5$.  For even $d$,
   157	sign already kills the infinity meridian, but an even permutation can still be a
   158	nontrivial value of $\rho(\gamma_\infty)$.  Thus sign-triviality must not be
   159	substituted for full INF-TRIVIAL.
   160	
   161	### 2.2 What full INF-TRIVIAL would give
   162	
   163	Let $\gamma_\infty$ be a meridian of $L_\infty$ in the affine complement.  If
   164	
   165	\[
   166	                         \rho(\gamma_\infty)=1,                    \tag{2.3}
   167	\]
   168	
   169	then (2.1) descends to $\pi_1(\mathbb P^2-\bar D)$.  Algebraic Riemann existence,
   170	followed by normalization of $\mathbb P^2$ in the associated index-five field,
   171	gives a connected normal finite map $f:X\to\mathbb P^2$ of degree five, unramified
   172	off $\bar D$, with generic
   173	inertia a transposition.  There is no divisorial ramification on $L_\infty$.
   174	Because a normal surface is Cohen--Macaulay and the base is regular of dimension
   175	two, this finite map is flat.  Writing
   176	$f_*\mathcal O_X=\mathcal O\oplus E^\vee$ with $\operatorname{rk}E=4$, the trace
   177	discriminant gives, because one-transposition inertia has tame discriminant
   178	valuation one and hence reduced divisor $\bar D$,
   179	
   180	\[
   181	                  \mathcal O_{\mathbb P^2}(\bar D)\simeq(\det E)^2. \tag{2.4}
   182	\]
   183	
   184	Hence $d$ must be even, the same obstruction obtained from
   185	$\operatorname{sgn}\rho(\gamma_\infty)=(-1)^d$.  For even $d$, (2.4) is not a
   186	contradiction.
   187	
   188	Miranda's binary-cubic theory is intrinsically degree three and supplies no
   189	quintic branch identity.  Casnati's degree-five theory, in its modern
   190	Casnati--Ekedahl form, says under the additional Gorenstein hypothesis that $E$
   191	of rank four, a rank-five bundle $F$, and an alternating section determine a
   192	Pfaffian resolution (five $4\times4$ Pfaffians).  It is structure data for covers,
   193	not a theorem that their branch divisor has a scalar torus form.  Normality does
   194	not by itself make the isolated points over $P_\infty$ Gorenstein.  Thus the
   195	direct replacement is the exact question
   196	
   197	```text
   198	OPEN[N5-S2-QUINTIC-PLANE]:
   199	  assuming rho(gamma_infinity)=1, exclude a normal flat degree-5 cover
   200	  X -> P2 with Galois closure S5 and simple branch divisor Dbar, where D is
   201	  rational, one-place, and has only two-smooth-branch affine singularities;
   202	  first prove the cover Gorenstein at infinity or extend the Pfaffian theory,
   203	  then classify the allowed Pfaffian discriminants.
   204	```
   205	
   206	No such classification is present in the reviewed inputs.  Chisini-type results,
   207	even when their smooth-source/generic-branch hypotheses hold, give uniqueness from
   208	a branch curve rather than nonexistence and therefore do not replace it.
   209	
