   116	## 2. THEOREM[S5-COPRIME-KILL]: Hurwitz/cycle machinery at S₅
   117	
   118	### 2.1 Orbit enumeration: which ⟨g⟩-conjugacy orbits of transpositions generate S₅?
   119	
   120	Assume `g=gcd(d,n)=1` in affine normal form (3.1), with a geometric
   121	transposition tuple `τ=(τ_1,…,τ_d)` generating `S_5` and
   122	`Π=τ_1…τ_d`. After a generic shear, the coprime infinity braid is
   123	`β_∞=δ_d^n` with `δ_d=σ_1…σ_{d-1}` (leading phases
   124	`n(θ+2πj)/d` stay separated; exponent `n(d-1)` matches
   125	`e=d-1+2δ_aff` via `δ_aff=(n-1)(d-1)/2`). Sol uses the right Hurwitz
   126	convention `(x,y)·σ=(y,y^{-1}xy)` and the inverse
   127	`δ̂_d:=δ_d^{-1}`. Direct check at `d=2,3`:
   128	
   129	```text
   130	τ · δ̂_d = (Π τ_d Π^{-1}, τ_1, …, τ_{d-1}).
   131	```
   132	
   133	Fixedness by `δ̂_d^n` is equivalent to fixedness by `δ_d^n`. Then
   134	`δ̂_d^d` conjugates every entry by `Π`, so `δ̂_d^{nd}`-fixedness says
   135	`Π^n` centralizes `⟨τ_i⟩=S_5`. The centre is trivial, hence
   136	`Π^n=1` (5.1). Sign: `Π` is a product of `d` transpositions, so
   137	`sgn Π=(-1)^d`.
   138	
   139	Extend `τ_{j-d}:=Π τ_j Π^{-1}`. Fixedness is `τ_j=τ_{j-n}`: the
   140	bi-infinite sequence is `n`-periodic, and translation by `d` is
   141	conjugation by `Π`. Because `gcd(d,n)=1`, translation by `d` is
   142	transitive on `Z/nZ`, so all entries form a single `⟨Π⟩`-conjugacy
   143	orbit of one transposition. Conjugation acts on edges by
   144	`Π·(ab)=(Π(a) Π(b))`. The generated group is `S_5` iff that edge
   145	orbit is a connected graph on five vertices.
   146	
   147	The seven conjugacy classes of `S_5`, with `Π` acting on a 2-set:
   148	
   149	| type of `Π` | edge orbits that meet 5 vertices | generates `S_5`? |
   150	|---|---|---|
   151	| `1` | single edges | no |
   152	| `(2)` | size ≤2; stars on 3 letters | no |
   153	| `(2^2)` | e.g. `{(13),(24)}` on 4 letters; `{(15),(25)}` on 3 | no |
   154	| `(3)(1^2)` | triangle on the 3-support; star from one fixed letter misses the other | no |
   155	| `(3)(2)` | the six edges of `K_{3,2}` between the 3-support and the 2-support | yes |
   156	| `(4)(1)` | four-edge star from the fixed letter | yes |
   157	| `(5)` | the 5-cycle of adjacent transpositions, or the diagonal 5-cycle | yes |
   158	
   159	For `(3)(2)=(123)(45)`, the orbit of `(14)` is
   160	`(14)→(25)→(34)→(15)→(24)→(35)`. For `(4)(1)=(1234)`, the orbit of
   161	`(15)` is the star at 5. For `(5)=(12345)`, `(12)` gives the boundary
   162	cycle and `(13)` the pentagram. A connected transposition graph on
   163	`n` vertices generates `S_n` (a spanning tree already does). Sol's
   164	table (`SOL:403-413`) matches this enumeration exactly. It is not an
   165	`S_4` subscript change: in `S_4` a 3-cycle's star from the unique
   166	fixed letter meets all four vertices, which is why Theorem A at `S_4`
   167	admits `ord Π=3`; here a 3-cycle has two fixed letters and cannot.
   168	
   169	### 2.2 Π-order fork
   170	
   171	`Π^n=1` forces `ord(Π)∣n`. Combined with the surviving types:
   172	
   173	- `(3)(2)` has order 6, so `6∣n`;
   174	- `(4)(1)` has order 4, so `4∣n`;
   175	- `(5)` has order 5, so `5∣n`.
   176	
   177	In particular `n≥4` (none of 1, 2, 3 is a multiple of 4, 5, or 6).
   178	Together with `d>n` this gives `d≥5`. This lower bound is
   179	load-bearing for the nodalization: it is the S5 replacement of
   180	Theorem A's `n≥3`.
   181	
   182	### 2.3 Parity constraints
   183	
   184	`sgn Π=(-1)^d`. A 3-cycle is even and a transposition odd, so
   185	`(3)(2)` is odd and `d` is odd. A 4-cycle is odd, so `(4)(1)` needs
   186	`d` odd. A 5-cycle is even, so `(5)` needs `d` even. This is Sol's
   187	last column. No generating type has both `d` and `n` odd: the first
   188	two force `n` even, the third forces `d` even. The S4 both-odd kill
   189	is thus included, but is not the mechanism.
   190	
   191	Coprime formulae (4.4) are the standard one-pair identities
   192	`δ_∞=(d-n-1)(d-1)/2`, `δ_aff=(n-1)(d-1)/2`, and
   193	`M_∞=mult+β_h-1=(d-n)+d-1=2d-n-1` (chart `(v,u)=(ζ^{d-n},ζ^d)`).
   194	Cross-check against the promoted identity
   195	`C'^2-2δ_aff=3d-2-M_∞` (`REV:130-134`): the coprime nodal Nori
   196	quantity is `B=n+d-1`, hence `M_∞=2d-n-1`, and N-A-RES
   197	`M_∞≤3d-3` is exactly `B>0`.
